-- Project configuration
--
-- TWO IMPLEMENTATIONS, TWO BUILDS, and the domain already has the shape for it:
-- `Project.language` / `Project.build` are `Option[T = String]` — ONE each — because
-- the multi-language carrier is `Module`, whose `language` is required and whose
-- `build` is its own. So the project-level pair names the PRIMARY (rustland is the
-- reference implementation) and the modules state the rest.
--
-- `tools` lists both test gates. A work item's `acceptance` names the ONE that gates
-- it: `cargo-test` for rustland work, `scaland-sbt-test` for scaland's.
--
-- UNTIL WI-1068, PASS THE FLAG BY HAND. `anthill-todo add` defaults to a single
-- `cargo-test` when `--acceptance` is not passed (`resolve_acceptance` in the bundled
-- main.anthill) and reads neither this list nor the modules below, so a scaland ticket
-- needs `--acceptance scaland-sbt-test` written out. Twenty-five were filed without it
-- and corrected in bulk. The wrong entry is not loud: a scala-gen change cannot fail
-- the Rust suite, so the recorded gate passes while measuring nothing the ticket is
-- about — which is why WI-1068 is about `add` and not about this file.

fact Project(
  name: "anthill",
  language: "rust",
  build: "cargo",
  modules: ["rustland", "scaland"],
  tools: ["cargo-test", "scaland-sbt-test"])

fact Module(name: "rustland", root: "rustland", language: "rust", build: "cargo")
fact Module(name: "scaland", root: "scaland", language: "scala", build: "sbt")
